# Native test harness

Run `pio test -e native`. PlatformIO builds every production source file with
`test_build_src = yes`, then executes mock-backed Unity tests without hardware.

Run `commands/regression-test` for native source coverage. It cleans only native
build artifacts, runs every test suite with GCC `--coverage`, prints a terminal
summary, and writes the HTML details report to `coverage/index.html`.
gcovr excludes compiler-generated `(throw)` exception edges (allocation-failure
paths that unit tests cannot trigger); branch totals reflect that exclusion.

Test suites are grouped one per production class (converters, timers, button,
DC/BLDC motors, controllers, encoders and I2C devices, GPS/IMU sensors, data
types, storage, multi-reset, feedback control). Shared mock-driven helpers live
in `test/support/NativeTestCommon.h`; `test/mocks` provides only
APIs called by production code and keeps GPIO, PWM, time, serial, I2C, and
filesystem state inspectable.

`syncClockTimeStamp` remains compile-covered only: its failed-time path waits
forever by design. Native tests do not invoke that path, preserving production
behavior.

Mocks script library-facing states only: I2C addresses, transactions and short
reads; BNO08x initialization/report/reset/event outcomes; LittleFS
open/read/write/content outcomes; and TinyGPS++ location-field state. GPS
callback tests follow GPSSensor's current `isLocationUpdated()` condition; the
TinyGPS++ mock does not parse NMEA or validate upstream TinyGPS++ behavior.
This suite does not
validate hardware timing, electrical PWM waveforms, physical I2C buses, sensor
firmware, filesystem media, or any hardware integration.
